1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,128
You close the laptop. Your brain does not.

2
00:00:04,728 --> 00:00:09,909
It's late in the evening. You are technically off duty, and somehow,

3
00:00:09,909 --> 00:00:13,261
you are still writing an email in your head.

4
00:00:13,861 --> 00:00:18,377
Your phone is face down. Which feels very disciplined.

5
00:00:18,377 --> 00:00:23,227
Except you have checked it twice in the last four minutes.

6
00:00:23,827 --> 00:00:29,265
If that sounds familiar, here are seven small breaks for stress.

7
00:00:29,265 --> 00:00:34,109
Simple ways to interrupt that feeling of always being on.

8
00:00:40,909 --> 00:00:43,021
So why is switching off so hard?

9
00:00:43,621 --> 00:00:48,765
When your days are full of noise, open tabs and notifications,

10
00:00:48,765 --> 00:00:53,245
you get used to always having something to respond to.

11
00:00:53,245 --> 00:01:00,048
Quiet can feel strange by comparison. As though you have forgotten an appointment.

12
00:01:00,648 --> 00:01:07,405
Here is the thing. Switching off rarely happens all at once. A few minutes here.

13
00:01:07,405 --> 00:01:11,037
A different choice there. Nothing dramatic.

14
00:01:11,637 --> 00:01:19,116
So here are seven ordinary things to try. One for sleep, movement, food, nature,

15
00:01:19,116 --> 00:01:21,920
silence, connection, and play.

16
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:26,441
One. Sleep. Dim the lights, and pick up paper.

17
00:01:27,041 --> 00:01:28,128
In one study,

18
00:01:28,128 --> 00:01:35,988
people who read on a glowing e-reader for several hours before bed took longer to fall asleep,

19
00:01:35,988 --> 00:01:40,754
had less REM sleep, and felt less alert the next morning,

20
00:01:40,754 --> 00:01:43,681
than when they read a printed book.

21
00:01:44,281 --> 00:01:48,811
The pause. Thirty minutes before bed, dim the lights,

22
00:01:48,811 --> 00:01:53,426
and swap the screen for a paper book. Any book counts.

23
00:01:53,426 --> 00:01:58,297
And if thirty minutes feels impossible tonight, try five.

24
00:01:59,597 --> 00:02:03,288
Two. Movement. Walk with one open question.

25
00:02:03,888 --> 00:02:16,014
A Stanford study found that people came up with around sixty percent more ideas on a creative thinking task while walking than while sitting.

26
00:02:16,014 --> 00:02:20,400
It even worked on a treadmill, facing a blank wall.

27
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:27,745
The pause. Take a walk with one open question, and don't chase the answer.

28
00:02:27,745 --> 00:02:31,027
Think about it once. Then let it be.

29
00:02:31,627 --> 00:02:39,377
If you walk with a dog, watch them for a moment. They are doing exactly one thing.

30
00:02:39,377 --> 00:02:46,560
Sniffing. Slowly, thoroughly. Not in the past. Not in the future. Just here.

31
00:02:47,860 --> 00:02:50,847
Three. Nourishment. One new plant.

32
00:02:51,447 --> 00:02:54,340
In a large study of gut bacteria,

33
00:02:54,340 --> 00:03:05,121
people who ate more than thirty different plants a week had a more diverse gut microbiome than people who ate ten or fewer.

34
00:03:05,721 --> 00:03:12,398
Thirty sounds like a lot. Until you start counting. A handful of walnuts.

35
00:03:12,398 --> 00:03:14,959
Some parsley. A lentil soup.

36
00:03:15,559 --> 00:03:19,341
The pause. Add one new plant to a meal today.

37
00:03:19,341 --> 00:03:25,308
Put your phone down for the first few bites. There is no score to keep.

38
00:03:26,608 --> 00:03:30,171
Four. Nature. Twenty minutes, no soundtrack.

39
00:03:30,771 --> 00:03:41,036
Researchers found that twenty to thirty minutes in a place that felt like nature was linked with lower levels of cortisol,

40
00:03:41,036 --> 00:03:42,803
one marker of stress.

41
00:03:43,403 --> 00:03:50,697
The pause. Go outside for twenty minutes. A park counts. No music. No podcast.

42
00:03:50,697 --> 00:03:57,056
No goal. And if you only have three minutes today, take those three.

43
00:03:58,356 --> 00:04:02,068
Five. Stillness. Two minutes longer than comfortable.

44
00:04:02,668 --> 00:04:04,155
In one experiment,

45
00:04:04,155 --> 00:04:10,020
people were asked to sit alone with their thoughts for fifteen minutes.

46
00:04:10,020 --> 00:04:16,876
They could also give themselves a mild electric shock. Quite a few chose the shock.

47
00:04:17,476 --> 00:04:24,580
So if silence feels uncomfortable, you are in good company. Silence takes practice.

48
00:04:25,180 --> 00:04:30,963
The pause. Stay in silence two minutes longer than feels comfortable.

49
00:04:30,963 --> 00:04:35,825
When you feel the urge to reach for your phone, notice it.

50
00:04:35,825 --> 00:04:38,004
Then stay a little longer.

51
00:04:38,004 --> 00:04:42,865
And don't force it if it ever feels genuinely distressing.

52
00:04:44,165 --> 00:04:46,683
Six. Connection. The coffee chat.

53
00:04:47,283 --> 00:04:53,002
In one study, coffee shop customers who smiled and had a short,

54
00:04:53,002 --> 00:04:59,993
genuine chat with the barista left in a better mood, and felt more connected,

55
00:04:59,993 --> 00:05:03,987
than those who kept it as quick as possible.

56
00:05:04,587 --> 00:05:11,536
The pause. When you buy your coffee, smile, make eye contact, and have a short chat.

57
00:05:11,536 --> 00:05:15,093
Thirty seconds about the weather is enough.

58
00:05:15,093 --> 00:05:20,139
It reminds you that there are real people outside your inbox.

59
00:05:21,439 --> 00:05:23,551
Seven. Play. Three good things.

60
00:05:24,151 --> 00:05:28,151
It is surprisingly easy to remember what went wrong,

61
00:05:28,151 --> 00:05:32,151
and forget the small things that were actually good.

62
00:05:32,751 --> 00:05:33,800
In one study,

63
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:42,031
people who wrote down three good things each evening for one week were still happier six months later.

64
00:05:42,631 --> 00:05:49,452
The pause. Write down three good things from today. They can be very small.

65
00:05:49,452 --> 00:05:56,455
Warm socks. A green light at the right moment. A message that made you smile.

66
00:05:57,755 --> 00:05:59,461
You don't need all seven.

67
00:06:00,061 --> 00:06:03,197
Pick one pause for today. Just one.

68
00:06:03,797 --> 00:06:10,155
Choose the moment first. After lunch, on the way home, or just before bed.

69
00:06:10,755 --> 00:06:16,387
Start with two minutes. Phone face down, or better, in another room.

70
00:06:16,987 --> 00:06:20,315
Do only the pause. One thing at a time.

71
00:06:20,915 --> 00:06:27,549
Afterwards, notice one small change in your body. Shoulders. Breath. Jaw.

72
00:06:28,149 --> 00:06:31,925
Tomorrow, try it again. Or choose a different one.

73
00:06:33,225 --> 00:06:39,217
One small warning. The first pauses may feel restless. A bit flat.

74
00:06:39,217 --> 00:06:41,033
Even a little empty.

75
00:06:41,633 --> 00:06:47,685
That's normal. Often it simply means you have been running for a long time,

76
00:06:47,685 --> 00:06:52,608
and the quiet finally has room to show you how tired you are.

77
00:06:52,608 --> 00:06:54,625
Be patient with yourself.

78
00:06:55,225 --> 00:07:00,678
You don't have to become a different person to learn how to switch off.

79
00:07:00,678 --> 00:07:05,593
A few minutes a day that belong only to you are enough to start.

80
00:07:06,893 --> 00:07:11,025
If choosing feels like one decision too many,

81
00:07:11,025 --> 00:07:17,453
there is a new free pause every day at pause-to-play.com. Try today's.

82
00:07:17,453 --> 00:07:19,565
And let that be enough.
